  <title>Babble Help</title>

  <section id="start" style="2column">
    <title>Getting Started</title>
    <p>
      Babble is a Mastodon client. It reads and posts on any Mastodon
      server, and on the other servers that server federates with. Start by
      typing your server's address on the welcome screen and choosing
      <gui>Continue</gui> or <gui>Continue with…</gui>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Your browser opens the server's own sign-in page. Babble never sees
      your password: the server hands back a token, and only that token is
      stored, in your system keyring.
    </p>
    <note style="important">
      <title>Ask only once</title>
      <p>
        Babble asks for the <sys>read write</sys> scopes. Moderation
        permissions are requested separately, and only when you open the
        Admin Dashboard.
      </p>
    </note>
    <p>
      If the browser does not come back on its own, Babble offers an
      <gui>Enter Authorization Code…</gui> button so you can paste the code
      the server shows you by hand.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="reading" style="2column">
    <title>Reading</title>
    <p>
      The places in the sidebar are the feeds you read. <gui>Home</gui> is
      the timeline of the accounts you follow, <gui>Notifications</gui>
      collects mentions and the rest, and <gui>Conversations</gui> holds
      direct messages. <gui>Local</gui> and <gui>Federated</gui> are your
      server's public timelines.
    </p>
    <p>
      With live updates on, new posts arrive as they are posted. When the
      stream cannot connect, Babble keeps polling and catches up on what it
      missed when the connection returns.
    </p>
    <p>
      <gui>Catch Up</gui> is the reading-back feature: pick a window of time,
      and it ranks the posts you missed by how much they are worth your
      attention, keeping that run frozen as a snapshot you can page through.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every list keeps its place: scrolling position and loaded posts
      survive switching away and coming back.
    </p>
    <p>
      Links normally open in your browser. Turn on <gui>Open Web Links
      Beside the Timeline</gui> in Preferences and they open in a column
      next to the timeline instead, or over it when the window is narrow.
      Links to posts and profiles still open in Babble itself, and
      Ctrl-click or a middle click sends a link to the browser.
    </p>
    <p>
      Pages in the column keep no cookies or history once Babble closes,
      cannot ask for your location, camera or microphone, and never
      download files: a download is handed to your browser.
      <gui>Open in Browser</gui> in the column's menu does the same for the
      page you are reading.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="writing" style="2column">
    <title>Writing</title>
    <p>
      <gui>New Post</gui>, or Ctrl+N, opens the composer. It checks spelling,
      completes <gui>@mentions</gui> and hashtags as you
      type, and accepts images, video and audio up to what your server
      allows.
    </p>
    <p>
      Attach media by choosing it in the composer, by pasting or dragging
      it in, or by taking a screenshot from the clipboard. Posts can be
      scheduled, and drafts are kept for you: close the composer with
      something half-written and Babble offers it back on the next launch.
    </p>
    <p>
      Ctrl+Enter sends. Escape closes the composer without sending.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="desktop" style="2column">
    <title>Desktop Integration</title>
    <p>
      Babble registers as a handler for <sys>babble://</sys> login
      redirects, for <sys>web+ap://</sys> object links and for
      <sys>web+mastodon://</sys> intents, so a link from another
      application opens here instead of in the browser.
    </p>
    <p>
      Files handed to Babble — an image chosen in your file manager, say —
      open in the composer, ready to post.
    </p>
    <p>
      Babble appears in the Activities overview search as a search provider,
      where you can look up a post without opening the window first.
    </p>
    <p>
      With <gui>Keep Running in the Background</gui> on, closing the window
      leaves Babble running so notifications still arrive; the dock icon
      brings the window back.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="settings" style="2column">
    <title>Settings</title>
    <p>
      Preferences opens with Ctrl+, and can be searched by typing. It covers
      the interface text size and line height, how posts refresh, desktop and
      server notifications, and the defaults a new post starts from.
    </p>
    <p>
      A few settings only reach as far as your server: default visibility,
      language and content warnings are stored in your server preferences,
      so they follow you to any other Mastodon client.
    </p>
    <p>
      Changing the interface language takes effect the next time Babble
      starts.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="problems" style="2column">
    <title>Common Problems</title>
    <p>
      If the computer has no network, Babble says so in a banner and keeps
      trying; the banner goes away by itself once the network is back.
    </p>
    <p>
      If a login disappears from the keyring, Babble forgets that account
      rather than retrying forever. Sign in again from the welcome screen.
    </p>
    <p>
      A server that does not support a feature hides it: Collections and the
      Admin Dashboard appear only where the server has them, so an older
      Mastodon shows a smaller menu rather than an error.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="keys" style="2column">
    <title>Keyboard</title>
    <p>
      Ctrl+N writes, Ctrl+F searches, Ctrl+R reloads, Ctrl+1 through Ctrl+9
      jump between places, Alt+Left goes back and Ctrl+, opens Preferences.
      Press <key>F1</key> for this help, or Ctrl+? to list every shortcut.
    </p>
    <p>
      While a page in the web column has the focus, Ctrl+R and F5 reload
      the page, Alt+Left and Alt+Right go back and forward, and Escape or
      Ctrl+W hides the column.
    </p>
  </section>

